  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1200 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nTuesday Night-Thursday...The next shortwave trough dives into \nthe Northern Plains Tuesday night, spawning a weak surface low \npressure. Unlike the previous trough passages, this one will \nhave a pocket of mid-level Pacific moisture associated with it. \nThe low levels are forecasted to still be dry, so top-down \nmoistening could produce some isolated rain showers (15-30% \ncoverage) on Wednesday. By Wednesday night, the trough continues\nto dig into the eastern CONUS, aided by an injection of shear \nvorticity on the backside of the longwave trough. The low \npressure quickly propagates southeastward across the Ohio River \nValley into the Mid-Atlantic as northerly flow pulls potentially\nthe coldest air of the season into the CWA. There is some \ndisagreement among the global models on some lingering low-level\nmoisture on Thursday. Latest LREF guidance shows a 40-60% \ndifference in 850 mb RH values between the 25th and 75th \npercentiles. If the low levels remain saturated, spotty rain \nshowers and drizzle could linger into Thursday night (10% \nchance).\n\nFriday-Saturday...High pressure settles into the Upper \nMississippi River Valley heading into the weekend. Mostly clear \nskies and dry, seasonable conditions return with highs in the \n40s and 50s and overnight low in the 30s.",
